196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (Developed nations hoarded vaccines which left conflict-affected states with low vaccination rates. Surveillance systems in war zones were too weak to detect outbreaks early and this crisis raised questions regarding global governance and accountability.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (The challenges encountered during the COVID-19 pandemic also resonate with previous experiences in managing Ebola Virus Disease \(EVD\) outbreaks. Lessons from treating EVD patients reveal that the virus remains a significant threat in several countries, contributing to persistent health security issues in vulnerable populations. Although considerable progress has been made in developing treatment methods and managing outbreaks, such as the recent cases in the Democratic Republic of Congo, the experiences of EVD outbreaks still serve as a critical lesson for combating new outbreaks like COVID-19. In particular, the occurrence of multiple Ebola outbreaks indicates the insufficiency of disease surveillance systems, laboratory capabilities, and general health infrastructure in conflict- affected regions, necessitating international collaboration to prevent and control future outbreaks. Moreover, the recent Hantavirus outbreak added to the list of concerning zoonotic diseases, drawing attention to the potential impacts of ecological damage, environmental change, and increased human activity on the spread of pathogens. Despite primarily affecting local populations, outbreaks of new viruses emphasize the importance of timely detection, comprehensive disease surveillance, and international collaboration in managing and preventing emerging health threats. Ultimately, the challenges posed by COVID-19, Ebola, and Hantavirus illustrate the need for improved healthcare systems, increased global health security, and better international relations to ensure an adequate response to future health crises. Therefore, it is evident that the challenges of pandemics cannot be addressed by one country alone, requiring the collaboration of various nations, international organizations, and entities such as the World Health Organization and the United Nations)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (The COVID-19 pandemic highlighted global disparities in vaccine distribution, contradicting the WHO's mandate to promote health equity. Of the 832 million COVID-19 vaccines administered early in the pandemic, 82% were distributed to high-income Member States. The already limited health services were disrupted when the pandemic hit, which caused a drop in global vaccine coverage for children under 12 by over 5%. An estimated 25 million children under the age of one missed important early childhood vaccines, the highest since 2009. There was a particular disparity where girls faced discrimination, with rates for important childhood vaccinations, like the HPV vaccine, dropping by over 10% from 2019 to 2020.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (Equity to Acquire Vaccines and Discrimination in Distribution)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
