202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (WHO has addressed this challenge with the COVID-19 Vaccine Global Access \(COVAX\) initiative to promote equitable distribution of vaccines across the globe. The Global Immunization Agenda 2030 prioritizes over 20 preventable diseases and focuses distribution mainly in infants across the developing world.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (Discrimination in vaccination during the COVID-19 pandemic was a critical failure of the global health order.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (The most significant form of discrimination occurred at the international level. High-income countries secured the majority of the early vaccine supply through bilateral deals \(a formal agreement between two parties that creates mutual promises and obligations\) with pharmaceutical companies. This resulted in a staggering global disparity, where 82% of vaccines were administered in high income countries early on and vaccination rates were approximately 1 in 4 in richer nations versus 1 in 500 in poorer nations.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (This discrimination in vaccination was not only a moral issue but also a significant health issue as large unvaccinated populations in developing nations acted as pool for viral mutation, threatening the efficacy)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (The effects of COVID-19 were magnified in states experiencing constant war or political fragility, pushing already strained health services toward total collapse. Countries which were in conflict such as Syria, Yemen, Afghanistan couldn't shift resources in response to the pandemic as they were dealing with war displacement. The already fragile medical supply chain shattered due to the border closing and global competition for PPE and ventilators. There was a large increase in preventable deaths; services such as maternal care dropped across 38% of member states, increasing stillbirth and maternal mortality rates in developing nations. There are millions of people who live in conflict zones as refugees in overcrowded camps where infectious diseases can spread rapidly. Due to the already collapsed immunization and sanitation programs in such areas, the people residing are at risk of various other diseases like measles, cholera and polio.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (The International Health Regulations \(IHR\) had critical flaws which were exposed during the initial phase of the pandemic. The IHR relied on individual states to quickly and transparently report a potential public health emergency of international concern \(PHEIC\), this clearly failed which led to a delayed recognition of the COVID-19 pandemic. WHO used the Early Warning, Alert, and Response System \(EWARS\). EWARS is a system which is designed to detect and rapidly respond to public health threats, particularly disease outbreaks, in emergency or vulnerable settings.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
