216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (of vaccines globally. To combat this, The COVAX initiative was launched by WHO, though it struggled to compete with the purchasing power of wealthy nations.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (Within countries, the preexisting social inequalities were further exacerbated when it came to vaccination. In many countries, racial and ethnic minority groups faced higher rates of infection, hospitalization, and death. Groups like refugees, internally displaced persons, and those in slums were consistently pushed to the back of the queue, making them highly vulnerable to outbreaks.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (It is evident that recurrent outbreaks have revealed critical healthcare system vulnerabilities, particularly in the Republic, where many of the outbreaks have occurred. These challenges include a lack of disease surveillance infrastructure, the capacity, and trained personnel in managing such cases, limited laboratory capacity, and insufficient infection prevention and control measures. In addition, several of these Ebola outbreaks occurred simultaneously with conflict and disaster events, with significant numbers of displaced persons and extensive socio-economic disruptions, making tracing and surveillance challenging. Moreover, each outbreak had distinct strains of the virus, which adds to the challenge. While there are now two authorized vaccines, Ervebo and Mvabea/Zabdeno, for the Zaire ebolavirus, there is no approved therapy for the Bundibugyo ebolavirus, and treatment is primarily supportive during an outbreak.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (Recurring Ebola Outbreaks)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
