>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (Hantavirus has become a major public health threat, with the problem being exacerbated by the fact that the virus is zoonotic, which means that the infection is transmitted from animals to humans. Unlike Ebola,)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (illnesses like malaria, typhoid fever, and other endemic conditions. The conflict, humanitarian crisis, displaced populations, and increased regional trade presented additional challenges to case detection, contact tracing, and healthcare access in the affected region. The WHO AFRO regional office initiated an epidemiological and laboratory investigation, emergency medical response, cross-border surveillance, and risk communication in coordination with neighboring states, humanitarian partners, and local authorities. The temporary recommendations adopted by the IHR \(2005\) Emergency Committee included advising Member States to step up surveillance at points of entry, rapidly identify sick travelers, and intensify laboratory testing and reporting while avoiding unnecessary interference with international traffic and trade.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (Ebola outbreaks have always revealed the weaknesses of the healthcare systems in affected countries. Firstly, the absence of Universal Health Coverage \(UHC\) often leaves the population without access to healthcare facilities under strain. The World Health Organization \(WHO\) reports that inadequate primary healthcare leads to delayed diagnoses, poor infection prevention and control, and an inability to isolate cases, resulting in further transmission before control measures can be effectively implemented. During the 2014-2016 outbreak, over 500 healthcare workers lost their lives to Ebola, severely impacting the response capacity of local health systems. Additionally, routine healthcare services such as childhood immunizations, maternal care, malaria treatment, HIV care, and TB treatment were significantly disrupted, leading to thousands of preventable deaths from other causes.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (A lack of diagnostic infrastructure, such as laboratories, is another significant challenge. The majority of countries are reliant on reference laboratories, leading to delays in diagnosis and treatment, as well as impeding contact tracing efforts. The WHO has called for decentralized testing and genomic surveillance to improve early detection and response capabilities. In addition, there is a critical shortage of intensive care, isolation, ambulances, medical oxygen, and essential medicines.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 145 0 R
/E (Finally, communities often suffer from fear, stigma, and mistrust of authorities, resulting in the concealment of cases and the failure to follow recommended safety measures. This can lead to the continued spread of the virus, as seen in various instances where individuals did not adhere to safe burial practices or seek treatment.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
