235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The 2026 MV Hondius hantavirus outbreak was one of the largest travel-related outbreaks of zoonoses and an example of how local health issues can become international emergencies due to modern transportation opportunities. The Dutch-registered expedition cruise ship called Hondius left Ushuaia, Argentina, on April 1, 2026, carrying 147 passengers and 25 crew members of 23 different nationalities. The vessel arrived at South Georgia and South Sandwich Islands, Antarctica, Tristan da Cunha, Saint Helena, Ascension, and returned to Ushuaia in early May. The World Health Organization \(WHO\) was alerted on May 2 nd , 2026, when the ship reached Tenerife, Canary Islands, after two passengers died, and another was hospitalized in severe condition. The infection was determined to be the Andes virus \(ANDV\), one of the hantaviruses that may be transmitted from person to person, on May 6 th .)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The response to the outbreak was complicated by the status of the cruise ship as a sovereign jurisdiction and the fact that no country had jurisdiction over the passengers of different nationalities. The response was coordinated by the World Health Organization, European Centre for Disease Prevention and Health Information, and the U.S. CDC in collaboration with national health authorities. The affected passengers were evacuated and repatriated via charter flights after the ship arrived in Tenerife, and those exposed to the virus underwent quarantine, while American citizens were sent to the Nebraska biocontainment unit. The response was challenging since it involved passengers of different nationalities, but the number of infected individuals was relatively small for such a large-scale initiative.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The international response to the 2026 MV Hondius outbreak was determined by the International Health Regulations \(2005\). In particular, member states are obliged to notify WHO of PHEIC, which may necessitate the adoption of emergency measures, and to provide it with appropriate information. Additionally, they should notify all states potentially affected by the event and collaborate on coordinating measures. In the context of the outbreak on the Hondius, the WHO issued Disease Outbreak News statements and conducted contact tracing. Moreover, the organization provided recommendations to states in collaboration with the CDC and ECDC, and ensured surveillance without restricting trade and travel between countries. The outbreak was officially over on July 20 th , 2026, after 13 cases of infection and three fatalities. The quarantine was lifted without the appearance of new cases.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The 2026 MV Hondius Hantavirus Outbreak)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
